MT=151 RESOLVED AND UNRESOLVED RESONANCE PARAMETERS               9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
  ----- RESOLVED RESONANCE PARAMETERS (1.e-5 ev - 20 keV) -----   9237 1451     
H. Derrien, A. Courcelle, L. C. Leal, N. Larson (ORNL, Oct 2004)  9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
   A new analysis of the resolved resonance region to 20 keV was  9237 1451     
performed at Oak Ridge National Laboratory, dated October, 2004.  9237 1451     
This evaluation was performed with the computer code SAMMY (La04) 9237 1451     
using Reich-Moore formalism. Resonance parameters are obtained    9237 1451     
from 0 to 20 keV. A description of the present evaluation can be  9237 1451     
found in (De04).                                                  9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
1. 1.e-5 eV to 1 keV                                              9237 1451     
   The thermal capture value was adjusted to sigma0 = 2.683 b     9237 1451     
(Trkov et al. [Tr04]). The shape of the capture cross-section in  9237 1451     
the thermal range was checked with the capture measuremements of  9237 1451     
Corvi et al. [Co87]. For the large s-wave 6.67 eV, 20.8 eV and    9237 1451     
36.6 eV resonances, the seven transmission spectra of Olsen       9237 1451     
[Ol77] were fitted with 4 transmission measurements of Meister et 9237 1451     
al. [Me98] and the capture measurements of De-Saussure et al      9237 1451     
[De73]. To get a better correction of residual errors in          9237 1451     
experimental normalizations and backgrounds, SAMMY fits were      9237 1451     
performed resonance by resonance up to 60 eV. The Crystal Lattice 9237 1451     
Model of SAMMY for Doppler broadening was used to describe the    9237 1451     
shape of the 6.67 eV resonance.                                   9237 1451     
   From 250 eV to 1 keV the Macklin et al. capture [Ma88]         9237 1451     
measurements were included in the fit. The spin of several p-wave 9237 1451     
resonances have been changed from the original evaluation         9237 1451     
([So94]) to account for the spin measurements of Gunsing et al.   9237 1451     
[Gu97]. Reliable estimates of p-wave neutron widths               9237 1451     
below 300 eV were given by Crawford et al. [Cr98] with very thick 9237 1451     
uranium sample transmission measurements and were used as prior   9237 1451     
parameters in the fit.                                            9237 1451     
   The fission widths (below 10 keV) are taken from ENDF\B-VI     9237 1451     
[So94] and were deduced from the Measurements of Defilippo et al. 9237 1451     
[De80]                                                            9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
 2.  1 keV to 20 keV                                              9237 1451     
   The Olsen 1979 transmission data [Ol79] were analyzed in the   9237 1451     
energy range 1 keV to 10 keV and the Harvey transmission data     9237 1451     
[Ha88] in the energy range 1 keV to 20 keV. The capture data of   9237 1451     
de Saussure[De73] and Macklin[Ma88] were analyzed in the energy   9237 1451     
range 1 keV to 10 keV and 1 keV to 20 keV respectively. In        9237 1451     
general, the thick sample transmissions calculated from the       9237 1451     
resonance parameters and averaged over 1 keV energy intervals     9237 1451     
agree within 1% with the experimental values of Harvey and within 9237 1451     
1.5% with the experimental values of Olsen, in agreement with the 9237 1451     
quoted experimental errors, The fit to the capture cross sections 9237 1451     
could not be obtained without large normalization and background  9237 1451     
corrections.                                                      9237 1451     
   The value of the effective scattering radius, R'=9.48 fm, and  9237 1451     
of the external resonance parameters, were obtained by assuming   9237 1451     
that the normalization coefficients in Harvey and Olsen           9237 1451     
experimental transmission were accurate within 1.0-1.5%. The      9237 1451     
statistical properties of the resonance parameters were checked   9237 1451     
against the Wigner distribution of the level spacing and the      9237 1451     
Porter-Thomas distribution of the reduced neutron widths.         9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
----- UNRESOLVED RESONANCE PARAMETERS (20 - 149 keV) -----        9237 1451     
   The ENDF/B-VI (Release 8) unresolved resonance parameter       9237 1451     
evaluation by Froehner and Poenitz (see Fr88,Po81) was adopted.   9237 1451     
