              ----- GENERAL INFORMATION -----                     9237 1451     
   The energy range of the evaluation (Yo04) was increased to 30  9237 1451     
Mev. Above the unresolved resonance region, new evaluations were  9237 1451     
performed of the neutron total, elastic, (n,n'), (n,2n), (n,3n),  9237 1451     
(n,4n), (n,f), (n,nf), (n,2nf), (n,4nf), and (n,gamma) cross      9237 1451     
sections, as well as their associated angular and energy          9237 1451     
distributions. The total (MT=1), (n,xn) (MT=16,17), fission       9237 1451     
(MT=18), and radiative capture (MT=102) cross sections are based  9237 1451     
mainly on experimental data, complimented by nuclear model        9237 1451     
calculations. Model parameters for the calculations were obtained 9237 1451     
by optimization with experimental data. The neutron total and     9237 1451     
(n,f) cross section revisions include new experimental data that  9237 1451     
were not included in the ENDF/B-VI 238U analysis.                 9237 1451     
   To provide the ENDF/B-VII data, a new theoretical analysis was 9237 1451     
performed. Coupled-channels optical model calculations were       9237 1451     
performed with the ECIS96 (Ra94) code for the lowest 3 members of 9237 1451     
the U238 ground state rotational band. These calculations were    9237 1451     
used to provide elastic and inelastic cross sections and angular  9237 1451     
distributions, and to provide neutron transmission coefficients   9237 1451     
for nuclear reaction theory calculations with the GNASH (Yo98)    9237 1451     
and COMNUC (Du71) Hauser-Feshbach statistical-fission-            9237 1451     
preequilibrium codes.  These model calculations were used to      9237 1451     
provide initial values of the MT=51-72 inelastic, (n,2n), (n,3n), 9237 1451     
and (n,f) cross sections, which were then modified somewhat to    9237 1451     
optimize agreement with experimental data. The coupled-channels   9237 1451     
optical model potential used is potential #5 in the International 9237 1451     
Atomic Energy Agency's Reference Input Parameter Library (RIPL-2) 9237 1451     
optical model parameter library (see Yo94).                       9237 1451     
   The GNASH calculations were used directly for the correlated   9237 1451     
energy-angle spectra in MF=6 for the (n,n'continuum), (n,2n),     9237 1451     
(n,3n), and (n,4n) reactions. The continuum data in MF=6          9237 1451     
incorporate the Kalbach systematics (Ka88) to specify the neutron 9237 1451     
angular distributions at given emission energies.                 9237 1451     
   The cross sections and angular distributions for MT=73-90 were 9237 1451     
obtained by calculations with ECIS96 of DWBA shapes from          9237 1451     
parameters obtained by matching the Baba (Ba89) neutron emission  9237 1451     
data at 14 MeV. These calculations assumed the existence of 2+    9237 1451     
and 3- vibrational states at excitation energies from 1.2 to 3.9  9237 1451     
MeV. Preequilibrium angular distribution shapes were used for     9237 1451     
MT=54-72 above 6 MeV.  Below 6 MeV, the MT=54-72 cross sections   9237 1451     
and angular distributions are combinations of compound nucleus    9237 1451     
and preequilibrium calculations. Some tuning for experimental     9237 1451     
data was done for the low-lying (n,n') level data.                9237 1451     
   The fission cross section (MT=18) from ENDF/B-VI was modified  9237 1451     
in the energy region above 1.0 MeV to reflect the new ENDF/B-VII  9237 1451     
238U(n,f) cross section obtained from the standards analysis of   9237 1451     
Pronyaev et al. (Pr05)  The modified cross section incorporates   9237 1451     
new fission cross section data, including that of Lisowski        9237 1451     
(Li02), and adjusts the cross section slightly higher from 1.3 -  9237 1451     
5.3 MeV.                                                          9237 1451     
   The present evaluation utilizes some data from the ENDF/B-VI   9237 1451     
evaluation. The total cross section below 6 MeV and the (n,f)     9237 1451     
cross section from 20 to 970 keV are taken from ENDF/B-VI.        9237 1451     
   The (n,5n) cross section becomes significant above about 25    9237 1451     
MeV.  Because there is no ENDF-6 MT number for this reaction, we  9237 1451     
have arbitrarily included this cross section in the (n,4n)        9237 1451     
reaction.  Consequently, the (n,4n) cross section becomes         9237 1451     
somewhat unphysical above about 25 MeV.                           9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
           ----- DETAILS OF LANL REVISION -----                   9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
MT=1: TOTAL CROSS SECTION                                         9237 1451     
   20 keV - 6 MeV:  Obtained from the ENDF/B-VI evaluation. In    9237 1451     
the unresolved resonance region (20 to 149 keV, the evaluation by 9237 1451     
F.H. Froehner (KFK - Fr88), which is based on a FITACS fit to     9237 1451     
experimental data, is used.                                       9237 1451     
   The results of a covariance analysis performed for the ENDF/B- 9237 1451     
VI evaluation are used from the unresolved energy region to 6     9237 1451     
MeV. The analysis was performed using the GLUCS code system       9237 1451     
(He80) and included the measurements of (Fo71), (Sc74), (Po81),   9237 1451     
(Po83), (Ha73),  (Br58), (Ca73), (Pe60), (Wh71), (Ba65), (Ut66),  9237 1451     
(Sh79), and (Li79). Data exclusions similar to the covariance     9237 1451     
analysis by Smith et al. [Sm82] were used, but additional data    9237 1451     
were included in the analysis.  The prior cross section for the   9237 1451     
analysis was taken from the coupled-channel analysis, described   9237 1451     
above.                                                            9237 1451     
   6 - 30 MeV:  The neutron total cross section from the          9237 1451     
covariance analysis was revised above En=6 MeV to agree more      9237 1451     
closely with the experimental data of Abfaulterer et al. [Ab00].  9237 1451     
The changes were generally a lowering of the cross section        9237 1451     
between 6 and 17 MeV, and an increase above 17 MeV.  The maximum  9237 1451     
change was about 1.7% but generally the changes were of the order 9237 1451     
of a few tenths of a percent.  Additionally, the Abfaulterer data 9237 1451     
were used to extend the evaluation from 20 to 30 MeV.             9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
MT=2: ELASTIC SCATTERING CROSS SECTION                            9237 1451     
   The elastic cross section is obtained by subtracting the       9237 1451     
nonelastic cross section (MT=3) from the total cross section      9237 1451     
(MT=1).                                                           9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
MT=3: NONELASTIC CROSS SECTION                                    9237 1451     
   The (redundant) nonelastic cross section is the sum of the     9237 1451     
following reactions: MT=4,16,17,18,37,102.                        9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
MT=4: INELASTIC CROSS SECTION                                     9237 1451     
   Sum of MT=51-91.                                               9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
MT=16: (n,2n) CROSS SECTION                                       9237 1451     
    Based on a combination of results from the GNASH calculations 9237 1451     
(see above), the covariance analysis used for the ENDF/B-VI       9237 1451     
analysis, and new experimental data.  The ENDF/B-VI covariance    9237 1451     
analysis included the measurements of (Ba66), (Fr80), (Ko80),     9237 1451     
(Yo78), (Ma69), (Ma72), (Ka79), (La73), (Ro57), (Ph56), (Pe61),   9237 1451     
(Ve78), (An58), and (Ry80). It utilized the GLUCS code of Hetrick 9237 1451     
and Fu (He80).                                                    9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
MT=17: (n,3n) CROSS SECTION                                       9237 1451     
   Same comment as MT=16. In this case the experimental data of   9237 1451     
(Fr80), (Wh62), (Ro57), (Ma69), (Al61), and (Ve78) were used in   9237 1451     
the covariance analysis.                                          9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
MT=18: FISSION NEUTRON CROSS SECTION                              9237 1451     
   20 keV - 1.0 MeV:  The fission cross section in this energy    9237 1451     
range is the same as ENDF/B-VI. That is, it is based on the       9237 1451     
unresolved resonance parameter analysis of Froehner and Poenitz   9237 1451     
(Fr88,Po81) and on the ENDF/B-VI standards analysis, which is     9237 1451     
similar to the ENDF/B-VII analysis at these energies.             9237 1451     
   1.0 - 30.0 MeV:  The fission cross section in the energy       9237 1451     
region above 1.0 MeV is based on results from the ENDF/B-VII      9237 1451     
standards cross section analysis of Pronyaev et al. (Pr05).  The  9237 1451     
238U(n,f) cross section is taken directly from the Version VII    9237 1451     
standards analysis. The original standards energy grid is         9237 1451     
included as a subset of a larger energy grid for the cross        9237 1451     
section.  The expansion to the denser grid was accomplished using 9237 1451     
a spline fit to a log-log file of the 238U(n,f) data from the     9237 1451     
standards analysis.                                               9237 1451     
   The Q-value was changed from 198.06 MeV to 198.032 MeV to      9237 1451     
maintain consistency with the MF=1,MT=458 data.                   9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
MT=19,20,21,38: MULTI-CHANCE FISSION CROSS SECTIONS               9237 1451     
   The ratios of the multi-chance fission cross sections to the   9237 1451     
total fission cross section were obtained from GNASH              9237 1451     
calculations.  The evaluated multi-chance fission cross sections  9237 1451     
were then obtained by multiplying the MT=18 total fission cross   9237 1451     
section by the ratios.                                            9237 1451     
   The Q-value was changed from 198.06 MeV to 198.032 MeV to      9237 1451     
maintain consistency with the MF=1,MT=458 data.                   9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
MT=37: (n,4n) CROSS SECTION                                       9237 1451     
   Threshold to 20 MeV, taken directly from the GNASH analysis    9237 1451     
described above.  Above about 25 MeV, it also includes the        9237 1451     
238U(n,5n) cross section.                                         9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
MT=51-71 DISCRETE INELASTIC LEVEL CROSS SECTIONS                  9237 1451     
   Coupled channel optical model calculations were performed with 9237 1451     
the ECIS96 code for the lowest 3 members of the U238 ground state 9237 1451     
rotational band (MT=2,51,52), including compound nucleus          9237 1451     
contributions. These calculations were also used to provide       9237 1451     
neutron transmission coefficients for nuclear reaction theory     9237 1451     
calculations with the GNASH (Yo98) and COMNUC (Du71) Hauser-      9237 1451     
Feshbach statistical-fission-preequilibrium codes.  See           9237 1451     
description above under GENERAL INFORMATION. These latter theory  9237 1451     
calculations were used to provide the MT=54-72 inelastic cross    9237 1451     
sections, and separate coupled-channels calculations were         9237 1451     
utilized for MT=53. Several of the low-lying level cross sections 9237 1451     
were modified somewhat, primarily to optimize agreement with      9237 1451     
experimental data.                                                9237 1451     
                                                                  9237 1451     
MT=72-90: DISCRETE INELASTIC LEVEL CROSS SECTIONS (DIRECT         9237 1451     
          REACTIONS TO GROUPS OF STATES)                          9237 1451     
   The cross section values in MT=72-90 are based upon Baba's     9237 1451     
neutron emission cross section data (Ba89) at 14 MeV for n + 238U 9237 1451     
reactions. To accomplish this, new DWBA calculations were run     9237 1451     
with the ECIS96 code, assuming the direct reactions occur mainly  9237 1451     
to 2+ or 3- vibrational states, and deformation parameters were   9237 1451     
determined by matching the 14-MeV Baba data.  The specific J,Pi   9237 1451     
assignments were made initially on the basis of the magnitude of  9237 1451     
the cross sections but were adjusted by trial and error to best   9237 1451     
accommodate the 14-MeV data. The cross section for each MT number 9237 1451     
in this range should be interpreted as the integrated cross       9237 1451     
section for several levels populated by direct reactions in the   9237 1451     
neighborhood of the excitation energy given for the MT number.    9237 1451     
   The DWBA calculations were used to extrapolate the 14-MeV      9237 1451     
cross sections to lower and higher energies, and to obtain the    9237 1451     
MF=4 angular distributions for each assumed state. The spins,     9237 1451     
parities, and deformation parameters used in the calculations are 9237 1451     
given in the table below. These results affect the evaluation in  9237 1451     
the excitation energy range Ex=1.17-3.91 MeV.                     9237 1451     
